  "query_preview": "..The Pacific Northwest low that has been moving\nacross northern Utah is now centered over central Wyoming this\nevening. Isolated showers and thunderstorms continued into the\nearly evening for northeastern portions of the forecast area, but\ngiven the loss of daytime heating and the eastward movement of the\nlow, showers have dissipated and skies are mostly clear for the\nvast majority of the area. Northern Utah and southwest Wyoming\nlocations saw much cooler temperatures today, with maxes averaging\n10F below seasonal normals. Over southern Utah, there was much\nless cooling, keeping temperatures near normal for this time of\nyear. \n\nHigh pressure is expected to move overhead tomorrow afternoon,\nwith Utah and southwest Wyoming sandwiched in between the exiting\nlow and another one approaching the California coast. As the ridge\nbuilds, maxes for northern portions of the area will increase by a\nfew degrees, running 5-7F above seasonal normals, with little\nchange over southern Utah. By Monday afternoon, the ridge axis is\non track to move into Colorado, with the resultant southerly flow\nbringing temperatures back to near normals across the entire area. \n\nSoutherly flow over the area is expected to increase by Monday\nnight as the California low makes its way into the Great Basin. In\nthis flow, monsoonal moisture is expected to advect back northward\ninto the area, with PWs expected to be in the 0.6 to 0.8 inch\nrange across much of the area by early Tuesday. Though confidence\nis high that there will be less moisture coming up with this push\nthan what was seen last week, it will still increase the potential\nof showers and thunderstorms across the area. There remains some\nspread in the guidance with regard to the system's track after it\nmoves into the Great Basin and how much it weakens while onshore.\nHowever, the majority of guidance is now indicating it will take a\nturn into southern Utah Monday night, moving across the remainder\nof the area in a weakened state during the day Tuesday. With this\nextra instability, the forecast for coverage of convection on\nTuesday is definitely trending upward. \n\nEnsemble spread increases further for the latter part of the\nupcoming week. Guidance is generally favoring a continued\nunsettled pattern for much of the remainder of the week, keeping\ntemperatures near normal with occasional chances of showers and\nthunderstorms. It should be noted that a minority of guidance,\nincluding the latest deterministic EC, show strong high pressure\nreturning by late in the upcoming week, bringing a warming and\ndrying trend.",
